      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.086,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "**If a net charge Q, flows across any cross-section of a conductor in time t, then the current I, through the cross-section is**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.128,
            0.897,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "I = \\frac { Q } { t }\\tag{11.1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.174,
            0.901,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "**The SI unit of electric charge is coulomb (C), which is equivalent to the charge contained in nearly** $6 \\times 1 0 ^ { 1 8 }$ **electrons. (We know that an electron possesses a negative charge of** $1 . 6   \\times   1 0 ^ { - 1 9 }   \\mathrm { C } . )$ **The electric current is expressed by a unit called ampere (A), named after the French scientist, Andre-Marie Ampere (1775–1836). One ampere is constituted by the flow of one coulomb of charge per second, that is,** $1   \\mathrm { A }   =   1   \\mathrm { C } / 1$ **s. Small quantities of current are expressed in milliampere**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.31,
            0.439,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.075,
                0.472,
                0.182,
                0.489
              ],
              "content": "Figure 11.1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.073,
                0.487,
                0.427,
                0.531
              ],
              "content": "A schematic diagram of an electric circuit comprising – cell, electric bulb, ammeter and plug key"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.453,
            0.298,
            0.901,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "(1 $\\mathrm { m A } = 1 0 ^ { - 3 }   \\mathrm { A } )$ or in microampere (1 $\\mu \\mathrm{A} = 10^{-6}   \\mathrm{A}$ **An instrument called ammeter measures electric current in a circuit. It is always connected in series in a circuit through which the current is to be measured. Figure 11.1 shows the schematic diagram of a typical electric circuit comprising a cell, an electric bulb, an ammeter and a plug key. Note that the electric current flows in the circuit from the positive terminal of the cell to the negative terminal of the cell through the bulb and ammeter.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.555,
            0.453,
            0.572
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 11.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.571,
            0.9,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "**A current of 0.5 A is drawn by a filament of an electric bulb for 10 minutes. Find the amount of electric charge that flows through the circuit.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.627,
            0.398,
            0.643
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solution"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.644,
            0.669,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "**We are given, I = 0.5 A; t = 10 min = 600 s.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.66,
            0.534,
            0.677
          ],
          "content": "**From Eq. (11.1), we have**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.675,
            0.486,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}Q_{ 氵 } \\stackrel{ 氵 }{=} I t \\\\\\stackrel{ 氵 }{=} 0.5  A  \\times 600  s  \\\\\\stackrel{ 氵 }{=} 300  C \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.749,
            0.749,
            0.777
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Q U E S T I O N S"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.802,
            0.446,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": "**1. What does an electric circuit mean?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.821,
            0.364,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "**2. Define the unit of current.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.839,
            0.717,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": "**3. Calculate the number of electrons constituting one coulomb of charge.**"
